# Logseq simple queries - {{query ...}} DSL
# Type /query. For the datalog form see logseq/datalog.
# https://docs.logseq.com/#/page/queries

## Operators (wrap any filters)
{{query (and [[page 1]] [[page 2]])}}          all must match
{{query (or [[page 1]] [[page 2]])}}           any may match
{{query (and [[tag2]] (not [[tag1]]))}}        exclude

## Block filters
[[page]]                       blocks referencing a page
"free text"                    full-text search (desktop app only)
(task now later)               by marker: now later todo doing done wait ...
(priority a)  (priority a b)   by priority
(property type book)           block property; matches page/tag values only
(page "term/alias")            blocks on a given page
(between -7d +7d)              journal blocks in a date range
(between -2w today)            symbols: today|yesterday|tomorrow|now
                               offsets: +|- N y|m|w|d|h|min
(between [[Dec 5th, 2020]] [[Dec 7th, 2020]])

## Page filters - cannot be mixed with block filters
(page-property related "Block embed")
(page-tags embed)
(all-page-tags)

## Sorting
(sort-by created-at desc)      order omitted means desc
(and (task now later) (sort-by created-at desc))
# created-at / updated-at only work for pages. Clicking a query-table column
# header sorts too, and that takes precedence over sort-by.

## Result display
query-table:: true                   render as a table (block property)
query-properties:: [:page :block]    which columns show
query-sort-by:: page
query-sort-desc:: false
:logseq.query/nlp-date:: true        parse "Aug 12th, 2022" values as dates
{{function (sum :price)}}            aggregate in a CHILD block of the query
{{function (average :price)}}        also: min, max, count
{{function (sum (map (fn [x] (* (:price x) (:qty x))) result))}}

## Gotchas
- Property filters match pages/tags inside a value: for
  `description:: I liked this #book`, `(property description book)` matches
  but `(property description liked)` does not.
- Page names are stored lower-cased.
- A simple query can also be the :query of an advanced query, e.g.
  {:title "DOING with priority A" :query (and (task doing) (priority a))}
